  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Potential for moderate to heavy rainfall and a  \nfew thunderstorms today and tonight with gusty winds...\ntransitioning to widespread rain and drizzle for Wednesday \ninto Thursday.\n\nA cold front has finally stalled to the south and lays west to\neast across North Carolina. The parent low pressure was \nlocated near the KY/VA border, with a surface trough extending \nto the north- northeast. Strong thunderstorms are ongoing along \nthis boundary with PWATs around 1.7 inches there. Sub-severe \nstorms and showers were ongoing east of there and into our \nforecast area, mainly west of Highway 220 or from west of a line\nfrom Hot Springs to Martinsville. To the east, northeast winds \nwere wedging weak high pressure into the area. Moderate rainfall\nrates and training storms have led to some Flood Advisories \nearly this morning. Anymore rain in isolated parts of Smyth to \nBland Counties and Yadkin to Surry Counties may warrant flash \nflood warnings. \n\nMid level troughing just to our west will keep a steady stream\nof short waves moving overhead. This combined with the main \nboundary wavering in the area through Thursday will bring \nnumerous chances for rain and a few thunderstorms. The main \nboundary, which should lift north again later today as a warm \nfront, and associated MUCAPE, should trigger additional \nthunderstorms and widespread showers for much of the area today \nand tonight. By late tonight into Wednesday and Thursday, \nthunder will be hard to come by, with moderate wedging having \nbuilt in across more of our forecast area. Thus we should \ntransition to more plain rain and drizzle.\n\nSPC has parts of SW VA amd much of NW NC in a Day 1 Marginal\nRisk contour; with a pulse storm/multicell environment that \nisn't unreasonable though severe threat is expected to remain \nisolated. A few storms could become briefly severe and produce \nisolated tree damage this afternoon and evening. PWATs are high \nfor this time of year and with slow storm motion we are in an \nSlight Risk for excessive rainfall for much of the Piedmont for \nthis afternoon and evening where flash-flooding may materialize.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Cooler weather and a dry forecast for the\nremainder of the week. \n\nENE winds and persistent cloud cover will support below normal \ntemperatures, most noticeable on Wednesday and Thursday with \nhighs struggling to warm out of the upper 50s/low 60s for the \nmountains, and the low to mid 60s for the Piedmont. We begin to \nrebound some by Friday into the weekend, and even more Monday \ninto Tuesday, although nothing warmer than 80F expected at this \ntime of the season. Along with this cool down, drier air and \nhigh pressure will bring an extended break in rain chances, \nmainly Thursday evening through through Tuesday.",
